-- Modified for Lean Pool.

module

public import LeanPool.FastDynamo.Analysis.ProductH2DenseEmbedding
public import LeanPool.FastDynamo.Dynamics.Assembly.ProductResolvent
public import LeanPool.FastDynamo.Dynamics.Assembly.SolenoidalSubspace
public import LeanPool.FastDynamo.Dynamics.HilleYosida.Generation
public import LeanPool.FastDynamo.Dynamics.HilleYosida.Invariance

/-!
# The all-frequency induction semigroup and its solenoidal restriction

`FastDynamo/Dynamics/Assembly/ProductResolvent.lean` constructs the Hille–Yosida ray
`productHilleYosidaRay u hu hε` of the all-frequency induction operator
`A = productInductionOperator u ε : DomainOperator ProductC3L2 ProductC3H2`, with the
fixed-sector constant `M_u = ‖transverseStretchingCoefficient u‖`, and
`FastDynamo/Analysis/ProductH2DenseEmbedding.lean` shows that the `H²` graph domain embeds
densely in `ProductC3L2`.  Those are exactly the two hypotheses of the abstract generation
theorem of `FastDynamo/Dynamics/HilleYosida/`, so this module turns them into a
`C₀`-semigroup and then cuts that semigroup down to the solenoidal subspace `L²_σ` built in
`FastDynamo/Dynamics/Assembly/SolenoidalSubspace.lean`.

## Contents

* **Generation on the full space.**  `productInductionSemigroup` is
  `HilleYosidaRay.semigroup` at the product ray, `productInductionRealization` the
  accompanying `DomainOperatorSemigroupRealization`: every `H²` datum evolves along a
  domain-valued forward strong solution of `A` whose embedding is the semigroup orbit.  The
  quantitative statements are `norm_productInductionSemigroup_le`, `‖S t‖ ≤ exp (M_u t)`, and
  `growthBound_productInductionSemigroup_le`.  The matching lower bound
  `re_le_growthBound_productInductionSemigroup_of_eigenpair` needs the
  uniqueness statement `productInductionOperator_forwardStrongSolutionsUnique`, which
  identifies the semigroup orbit of an eigenvector with the exponential trajectory.
  `productInductionSemigroup_unique` says that no other strongly continuous semigroup
  realizes `A`, so the construction does not depend on the chosen ray.

* **Invariance of `L²_σ`.**  `productInductionSemigroup_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace` feeds
  the resolvent invariance `stateResolvent_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace` — valid at every
  positive real spectral parameter of any resolvent certificate, and packaged for the product
  ray as `stateResolvent_productHilleYosidaRay_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace` — to
  `HilleYosidaRay.semigroup_mem_of_closed_resolvent_invariant`, which propagates it along the
  Yosida approximation scheme.  The positivity of the parameter is free: the ray starts at
  `M_u = ‖transverseStretchingCoefficient u‖ ≥ 0`.  Read on the domain side this is
  `productInductionRealization_domainOrbit_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace`, and with it
  `productL2ToHMinus2_domainOrbit_eq_physical`: the orbit of a solenoidal `H²` datum solves,
  at every nonnegative time, the paper's curl-form induction equation in `H⁻²`.

* **The restricted semigroup.**  `solenoidalInductionSemigroup` is
  `StronglyContinuousSemigroup.restrict` applied to that invariance, so its evolution
  operator is the ambient one read through the inclusion (`solenoidalInductionSemigroup_apply`)
  and the exponential bound and growth bound descend from the abstract restriction API of
  `FastDynamo/Dynamics/HilleYosida/Invariance.lean` (`norm_solenoidalInductionSemigroup_le`,
  `growthBound_solenoidalInductionSemigroup_le`).  Restriction can only *decrease* a growth
  bound, so the spectral lower bound has to be re-proved on the subspace rather than
  inherited: `exp_re_le_norm_solenoidalInductionSemigroup` evaluates the restricted operator
  on an eigenvector that happens to lie in `L²_σ` — no norm is lost, the subspace carrying the
  ambient norm — and `re_le_growthBound_solenoidalInductionSemigroup_of_eigenpair` reads off
  `Re λ ≤ (S|_{L²_σ}).growthBound`.

The generation theorem used here is of Hille–Yosida type, on the ambient space.  The generator
of the restricted semigroup is identified in
`FastDynamo/Dynamics/Assembly/SolenoidalGenerator.lean`;
analyticity, compactness and the spectral statements of `lem:semigroup-spectral-growth` are
proved in `FastDynamo/Dynamics/Assembly/InductionSemigroupSpectrum.lean` and collected in
`SemigroupSpectralGrowth`.
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace FastDynamo

noncomputable section

variable (u : TransverseRealW1Inf) (hu : IsTransverseDivergenceFree u) {ε : ℝ} (hε : 0 < ε)

/-! ## Generation on the full product `L²` -/

/--
**The all-frequency induction semigroup.**  For a transversely divergence-free `W^{1,∞}`
velocity and a positive diffusivity, the `C₀`-semigroup on `ProductC3L2` generated by the
auxiliary induction operator `A = productInductionOperator u ε`.

It is `HilleYosidaRay.semigroup` applied to the assembled ray `productHilleYosidaRay` and to
the density statement `denseRange_productInductionOperator_embedding`.  By
`productInductionSemigroup_unique` below it is the *only* strongly continuous semigroup
realizing `A`, so the definite article is earned and the choice of ray is immaterial.
-/
def productInductionSemigroup : StronglyContinuousSemigroup ProductC3L2 :=
  (productHilleYosidaRay u hu hε).semigroup (denseRange_productInductionOperator_embedding u ε)

/--
**The realization.**  The all-frequency induction semigroup realizes the all-frequency
induction operator: every `H²` datum `B` has a domain-valued forward strong solution of `A`
starting at `B` whose `L²` embedding is the semigroup orbit of `productH2Embedding B`.

This is `HilleYosidaRay.realization` at the product ray.  It is the object the growth-bound
theory of `FastDynamo/Dynamics/GrowthBound.lean` consumes, and the reason the growth rate
built from this semigroup is the growth rate *of the induction operator* rather than of an
unrelated evolution.
-/
def productInductionRealization :
    DomainOperatorSemigroupRealization (productInductionSemigroup u hu hε)
      (productInductionOperator u ε) :=
  (productHilleYosidaRay u hu hε).realization
    (denseRange_productInductionOperator_embedding u ε)

/--
**Uniqueness of the all-frequency induction semigroup.**  Every strongly continuous semigroup
on `ProductC3L2` that realizes `A = productInductionOperator u ε` — in the sense of carrying a
`DomainOperatorSemigroupRealization` — is equal to `productInductionSemigroup u hu hε`.

This is `HilleYosidaRay.semigroup_unique` at `productHilleYosidaRay`, whose two hypotheses hold
for every divergence-free `u` and `ε > 0`: `denseRange_productInductionOperator_embedding`
(density of the `H²` graph domain in `L²`) and
`productInductionOperator_forwardStrongSolutionsUnique` (uniqueness of forward strong solutions,
from the energy competition).

Two consequences.  The definite article of `productInductionSemigroup` is earned.  And the two
`Classical.choice` applications that `productHilleYosidaRay` inherits, through
`sectorResolventInv`, from `reducedInductionRightHalfPlaneResolvent` are inert:
a different choice of sector resolvent family yields a different `HilleYosidaRay`, but the same
semigroup.
-/
theorem productInductionSemigroup_unique {S : StronglyContinuousSemigroup ProductC3L2}
    (R : DomainOperatorSemigroupRealization S (productInductionOperator u ε)) :
    S = productInductionSemigroup u hu hε :=
  (productHilleYosidaRay u hu hε).semigroup_unique
    (denseRange_productInductionOperator_embedding u ε)
    (productInductionOperator_forwardStrongSolutionsUnique u hu hε.le) R

/-- **The exponential bound**: `‖S t‖ ≤ exp (M_u t)` with the fixed-sector constant
`M_u = ‖transverseStretchingCoefficient u‖`, uniformly in the diffusivity.  This is
`HilleYosidaRay.norm_semigroup_le` at the product ray. -/
theorem norm_productInductionSemigroup_le (t : NNReal) :
    ‖(productInductionSemigroup u hu hε).operator t‖ ≤
      Real.exp (‖transverseStretchingCoefficient u‖ * (t : ℝ)) :=
  (productHilleYosidaRay u hu hε).norm_semigroup_le
    (denseRange_productInductionOperator_embedding u ε) t

/-- **The growth ceiling on the full space**: the growth bound of the all-frequency induction
semigroup is at most `M_u = ‖transverseStretchingCoefficient u‖`.  This is
`HilleYosidaRay.growthBound_le` at the product ray, i.e. the exponential bound above fed to
`StronglyContinuousSemigroup.growthBound_le_of_norm_le`. -/
theorem growthBound_productInductionSemigroup_le :
    (productInductionSemigroup u hu hε).growthBound ≤
      ((‖transverseStretchingCoefficient u‖ : ℝ) : EReal) :=
  (productHilleYosidaRay u hu hε).growthBound_le
    (denseRange_productInductionOperator_embedding u ε)

/--
**The spectral lower bound on the full space.**  Every eigenvalue of the all-frequency
induction operator has real part at most the growth bound of the generated semigroup.

This is `re_le_growthBound_of_hilleYosidaRay_of_eigenpair`, whose uniqueness hypothesis is
`productInductionOperator_forwardStrongSolutionsUnique`: uniqueness identifies
the semigroup orbit of the eigenvector with the exponential trajectory `e^{λ t} B`, whose norm
grows exactly like `e^{t Re λ}`.  Nonvanishing of the eigenvector is part of
`DomainOperator.IsEigenpair`.
-/
theorem re_le_growthBound_productInductionSemigroup_of_eigenpair {lam : ℂ} {B : ProductC3H2}
    (hpair : (productInductionOperator u ε).IsEigenpair lam B) :
    (lam.re : EReal) ≤ (productInductionSemigroup u hu hε).growthBound :=
  (re_le_growthBound_of_hilleYosidaRay_of_eigenpair (productHilleYosidaRay u hu hε)
    (denseRange_productInductionOperator_embedding u ε)
    (productInductionOperator_forwardStrongSolutionsUnique u hu hε.le) hpair).1

/-! ## Invariance of the solenoidal subspace -/

/--
**The state resolvent of the product ray leaves `L²_σ` invariant.**  At every real parameter
`r` beyond the Hille–Yosida constant `M_u = ‖transverseStretchingCoefficient u‖`, the resolvent
certificate stored in `productHilleYosidaRay u hu hε` maps the solenoidal subspace into itself.

This is `stateResolvent_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace` at that certificate; the positivity of
the parameter which it needs is free, since `0 ≤ M_u < r`.  It is stated separately because it
is the single hypothesis of both
`HilleYosidaRay.semigroup_mem_of_closed_resolvent_invariant` and
`HilleYosidaRay.restrictedSemigroup`.
-/
theorem stateResolvent_productHilleYosidaRay_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace (r : ℝ)
    (hr : ‖transverseStretchingCoefficient u‖ < r) (y : ProductC3L2)
    (hy : y ∈ productSolenoidalSubspace) :
    (productHilleYosidaRay u hu hε).resolvent.stateResolvent (r : ℂ) y ∈
      productSolenoidalSubspace := by
  have hr0 : 0 < r := lt_of_le_of_lt (norm_nonneg _) hr
  exact stateResolvent_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace u hu hε.le
    (productHilleYosidaRay u hu hε).resolvent hr0
    ((productHilleYosidaRay u hu hε).mem_ofReal r hr) y hy

/--
**The solenoidal subspace is invariant under the induction semigroup.**  If `x ∈ L²_σ` then
`S t x ∈ L²_σ` at every nonnegative time.

The hypothesis of `HilleYosidaRay.semigroup_mem_of_closed_resolvent_invariant` is invariance
of the *state resolvent* along the real ray `M_u < r`, which is
`stateResolvent_productHilleYosidaRay_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace` above.  Closedness of
`L²_σ` is `isClosed_productSolenoidalSubspace`.  Invariance then propagates along the Yosida
approximation scheme, the only route available since the generated semigroup is a limit and
not a formula.
-/
theorem productInductionSemigroup_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace (t : NNReal)
    (x : ProductC3L2) (hx : x ∈ productSolenoidalSubspace) :
    productInductionSemigroup u hu hε t x ∈ productSolenoidalSubspace :=
  (productHilleYosidaRay u hu hε).semigroup_mem_of_closed_resolvent_invariant
    (denseRange_productInductionOperator_embedding u ε) productSolenoidalSubspace
    isClosed_productSolenoidalSubspace
    (stateResolvent_productHilleYosidaRay_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace u hu hε) t x hx

/--
**The orbit of a solenoidal `H²` datum is solenoidal at every nonnegative time.**  The
domain-valued representative `domainOrbit B` carried by `productInductionRealization` stays
inside `L²_σ` once it starts there.

Its embedding is the semigroup orbit
(`DomainOperatorSemigroupRealization.semigroup_eq_embedding_domainOrbit`), so this is
`productInductionSemigroup_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace` read on the domain side.
-/
theorem productInductionRealization_domainOrbit_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace
    (B : ProductC3H2) (hB : productH2Embedding B ∈ productSolenoidalSubspace)
    {t : ℝ} (ht : 0 ≤ t) :
    productH2Embedding ((productInductionRealization u hu hε).domainOrbit B t) ∈
      productSolenoidalSubspace := by
  have hemb : productH2Embedding ((productInductionRealization u hu hε).domainOrbit B t)
      = productInductionSemigroup u hu hε (Real.toNNReal t) (productH2Embedding B) :=
    ((productInductionRealization u hu hε).semigroup_eq_embedding_domainOrbit B t ht).symm
  rw [hemb]
  exact productInductionSemigroup_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace u hu hε _ _ hB

/--
**The orbits solve the paper's curl-form induction equation.**  For a solenoidal `H²`
datum `B` and every `t ≥ 0`, the auxiliary action of the generator on the domain orbit agrees,
in `H⁻²`, with the paper's curl form `L_{ε,u} B = curl (u × B) + ε Δ B`:

`ι (A (domainOrbit B t)) = L_{ε,u} (domainOrbit B t)`.

Together with `productInductionRealization_domainOrbit_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace` this says
that every solenoidal `H²` datum evolves along a curve which at every nonnegative time is a
solenoidal `H²` field *and* solves the paper's equation.  It is
`productInductionAction_toHMinus2_eq_physical` (`FastDynamo/Operator/Compatibility.lean`)
applied to the orbit, whose divergence-freeness is exactly what the invariance statement
supplies.

The identification of the restricted semigroup with the semigroup generated on `L²_σ` by the
paper's `L_{ε,u}` with domain `H² ∩ L²_σ` is `generator_solenoidalInductionSemigroup`
(`FastDynamo/Dynamics/Assembly/SolenoidalGenerator.lean`), through the part of the generator in
the invariant subspace (Engel–Nagel II.2.3); that the orbit is the only solution of the
curl-form equation is `productH2Embedding_eq_productInductionSemigroup_of_isForwardStrongSolution`
(`FastDynamo/Dynamics/Assembly/PhysicalInductionUniqueness.lean`).
-/
theorem productL2ToHMinus2_domainOrbit_eq_physical
    (B : ProductC3H2) (hB : productH2Embedding B ∈ productSolenoidalSubspace)
    {t : ℝ} (ht : 0 ≤ t) :
    productL2ToHMinus2 (productInductionAction u ε
        ((productInductionRealization u hu hε).domainOrbit B t))
      = productPhysicalInductionAction u ε
        ((productInductionRealization u hu hε).domainOrbit B t) :=
  productInductionAction_toHMinus2_eq_physical u hu ε _
    ((productDivergenceFromH2_eq_zero_iff _).2 ((mem_productSolenoidalSubspace_iff _).1
      (productInductionRealization_domainOrbit_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace u hu hε B hB ht)))

/-! ## The semigroup on the solenoidal subspace -/

/--
**The induction semigroup on `L²_σ`.**  The restriction of the all-frequency induction
semigroup to the closed solenoidal subspace, which it leaves invariant.

This is the semigroup whose growth bound is the paper's `γ(u, ε)` once transported to the
literal three-torus (`FastDynamo/Dynamics/Assembly/MainGrowthRate.lean`): the paper's
state space is `L²_σ`, and passing to an invariant subspace can strictly lower a growth bound,
so a bound on the ambient growth rate is no evidence about this one.  Its generator is the
paper's `L_{ε,u}` on `L²_σ` with domain `H² ∩ L²_σ` (`generator_solenoidalInductionSemigroup`).
-/
def solenoidalInductionSemigroup : StronglyContinuousSemigroup productSolenoidalSubspace :=
  (productInductionSemigroup u hu hε).restrict productSolenoidalSubspace
    (productInductionSemigroup_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace u hu hε)

/-- The restricted evolution operator is the ambient one, read through the inclusion of `L²_σ`
into `ProductC3L2`.  This is definitional. -/
@[simp] theorem solenoidalInductionSemigroup_apply (t : NNReal)
    (x : productSolenoidalSubspace) :
    ((solenoidalInductionSemigroup u hu hε) t x : ProductC3L2) =
      productInductionSemigroup u hu hε t x :=
  rfl

/-- The exponential bound survives the restriction: `‖S|_{L²_σ} t‖ ≤ exp (M_u t)`.  Restricting
cannot increase an operator norm (`StronglyContinuousSemigroup.norm_restrict_le`). -/
theorem norm_solenoidalInductionSemigroup_le (t : NNReal) :
    ‖(solenoidalInductionSemigroup u hu hε).operator t‖ ≤
      Real.exp (‖transverseStretchingCoefficient u‖ * (t : ℝ)) :=
  (productHilleYosidaRay u hu hε).norm_restrictedSemigroup_le
    (denseRange_productInductionOperator_embedding u ε) productSolenoidalSubspace
    isClosed_productSolenoidalSubspace
    (stateResolvent_productHilleYosidaRay_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace u hu hε) t

/-- **The growth ceiling on `L²_σ`**: the growth bound of the restricted semigroup is at most
`M_u = ‖transverseStretchingCoefficient u‖`.  Restriction can only decrease a growth bound
(`StronglyContinuousSemigroup.growthBound_restrict_le`), and the ambient one is already
bounded by `M_u`. -/
theorem growthBound_solenoidalInductionSemigroup_le :
    (solenoidalInductionSemigroup u hu hε).growthBound ≤
      ((‖transverseStretchingCoefficient u‖ : ℝ) : EReal) :=
  (productHilleYosidaRay u hu hε).growthBound_restrictedSemigroup_le
    (denseRange_productInductionOperator_embedding u ε) productSolenoidalSubspace
    isClosed_productSolenoidalSubspace
    (stateResolvent_productHilleYosidaRay_mem_productSolenoidalSubspace u hu hε)

/--
**The eigen-orbit lower bound on the restricted semigroup.**  If `(λ, B)` is an eigenpair of
the all-frequency induction operator whose `L²` field is solenoidal, then the restricted
operator norm is at least `e^{t Re λ}` at every time.

The eigenvector `⟨ι B, hB⟩` is a *vector of the subspace* whose norm is the ambient norm, so
evaluating the restricted operator on it loses nothing: its image is the ambient orbit, whose
norm is exactly `e^{t Re λ} ‖ι B‖` by
`DomainOperatorSemigroupRealization.norm_apply_embedding_eigenvector` applied to
`productInductionRealization` and the uniqueness statement.  Dividing by the
positive number `‖ι B‖` — nonvanishing is part of `DomainOperator.IsEigenpair` — through
`ContinuousLinearMap.le_opNorm` gives the claim, so no separate normalisation of the
eigenvector is needed.

The statement cannot be obtained from `re_le_growthBound_productInductionSemigroup_of_eigenpair`
by restriction: that lemma is about the ambient semigroup and restriction goes the wrong way.
-/
theorem exp_re_le_norm_solenoidalInductionSemigroup {lam : ℂ} {B : ProductC3H2}
    (hpair : (productInductionOperator u ε).IsEigenpair lam B)
    (hB : productH2Embedding B ∈ productSolenoidalSubspace) (t : NNReal) :
    Real.exp ((t : ℝ) * lam.re) ≤ ‖(solenoidalInductionSemigroup u hu hε).operator t‖ := by
  have hBnorm : 0 < ‖productH2Embedding B‖ := norm_pos_iff.mpr hpair.1
  set v : productSolenoidalSubspace := ⟨productH2Embedding B, hB⟩ with hv
  have hvnorm : ‖v‖ = ‖productH2Embedding B‖ := rfl
  have horbit : ‖productInductionSemigroup u hu hε t (productH2Embedding B)‖ =
      Real.exp ((t : ℝ) * lam.re) * ‖productH2Embedding B‖ :=
    (productInductionRealization u hu hε).norm_apply_embedding_eigenvector
      (productInductionOperator_forwardStrongSolutionsUnique u hu hε.le) lam B hpair t
  have hcoe : ‖(solenoidalInductionSemigroup u hu hε).operator t v‖ =
      ‖productInductionSemigroup u hu hε t (productH2Embedding B)‖ := rfl
  refine le_of_mul_le_mul_right ?_ hBnorm
  calc Real.exp ((t : ℝ) * lam.re) * ‖productH2Embedding B‖
      = ‖(solenoidalInductionSemigroup u hu hε).operator t v‖ := by rw [hcoe, horbit]
    _ ≤ ‖(solenoidalInductionSemigroup u hu hε).operator t‖ * ‖v‖ :=
        ContinuousLinearMap.le_opNorm _ v
    _ = ‖(solenoidalInductionSemigroup u hu hε).operator t‖ * ‖productH2Embedding B‖ := by
        rw [hvnorm]

/--
**The spectral lower bound on `L²_σ`.**  An eigenvalue of the all-frequency induction operator
whose eigenfunction is solenoidal has real part at most the growth bound of the *restricted*
semigroup.

This is `exp_re_le_norm_solenoidalInductionSemigroup` fed to
`StronglyContinuousSemigroup.le_growthBound_of_norm_ge`, which only needs the exponential
minorant eventually along `atTop`.  It is the inequality `γ(u, ε) ≥ Re λ_ε` of the paper's
proof of `thm:main`, on the paper's own state space.
-/
theorem re_le_growthBound_solenoidalInductionSemigroup_of_eigenpair {lam : ℂ}
    {B : ProductC3H2} (hpair : (productInductionOperator u ε).IsEigenpair lam B)
    (hB : productH2Embedding B ∈ productSolenoidalSubspace) :
    (lam.re : EReal) ≤ (solenoidalInductionSemigroup u hu hε).growthBound := by
  refine (solenoidalInductionSemigroup u hu hε).le_growthBound_of_norm_ge ?_
  filter_upwards [Filter.eventually_ge_atTop (0 : ℝ)] with t ht
  have h := exp_re_le_norm_solenoidalInductionSemigroup u hu hε hpair hB (Real.toNNReal t)
  rwa [Real.coe_toNNReal t ht, mul_comm t lam.re] at h

end

end FastDynamo
